<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Soutpansberg</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Zoutpansberg</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Soutpansberg is a mountain range in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limpopo">Limpopo</a> province in the far north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>, named after a salt pan at its western end. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andries-hendrik-potgieter">Andries Hendrik Potgieter</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voortrekkers">Voortrekkers</a> settled at its foot in 1848, founding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schoemansdal">Schoemansdal</a>, the centre of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boer</a> republic later absorbed into the South African Republic. The region is home to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venda-people">Venda people</a> and is rich in plant and animal life.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/soutpansberg.jpg" alt="Soutpansberg" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Petrus Potgieter · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vivo_Limpopo_SouthAfrica_June2009.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The range lies in Vhembe District and runs some 107 kilometres east from the salt pan, known in Venda as Thavha ya muno, to the Matikwa Nature Reserve. As a whole it had no Venda name and was known by its sub-ranges, such as Dzanani and Songozwi. Two defiles cut through it: the Waterpoort in the west, carrying the Sand River and a railway, and Wyllie's Poort, which takes the N1 road from Louis Trichardt to Musina. Its highest peak is Lajuma at 1,747 metres, and the Nzhelele, Nwanedi, Levubu and Letaba rivers rise on its slopes.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first white man to reach and name the mountain was Coenraad de Buys, who fled Graaff Reinet after a failed rebellion in 1795 and settled near it in 1820, founding the clan known as the Buysvolk. The voortrekker Louis Tregardt camped at the salt pan in 1836 and near the later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schoemansdal">Schoemansdal</a> until 1837. The settlement of 1848, Zoutpansbergdorp, was founded by Jan Valentyn Botha, leading a faction of Andries Potgieter's trek; Potgieter died there in 1852, and in 1855 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stephanus-schoeman">Stephanus Schoeman</a> named the town Schoemansdal. By then it was an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>-trading centre of about 200 people, supplied by Venda hunters who received firearms in return.</p><p>Relations soured over taxes, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> raiding and the gun trade, and in 1866 the voortrekkers intervened in a Venda succession dispute; the claimant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/makhado">Makhado</a> attacked an outlying settlement, and the Venda mountain strongholds could not be taken. The voortrekkers abandoned the town on 15 July 1867. In 1898 General Piet Joubert's commando returned to regain control and burned Mphefu's royal village, and Trichardtsdorp, now Louis Trichardt, was proclaimed the next year.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The range is part of the Vhembe Biosphere Reserve, designated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> in 2009. About 2,500 to 3,000 vascular plant taxa grow there, 24 plant species are found nowhere else, and at least 116 reptile species have been recorded, among them the endemic Soutpansberg rock lizard and dwarf gecko.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Soutpansberg” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Britain annexed Natal in 1843 and proclaimed the Orange River Sovereignty in 1848, many trekkers moved north of the Vaal River and settled in scattered communities around Potchefstroom, Lydenburg, <b>Zoutpansberg</b> and Utrecht.</blockquote><a href="/empire/south-african-republic">South African Republic · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Its rulers brought stone-walling traditions and sacred drums, and they united the Venda of the <b>Soutpansberg</b> under one kingship.</blockquote><a href="/state/venda-kingdom">Venda Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>Andries Hendrik Potgieter led his followers north from Ohrigstad in 1848 to the <b>Soutpansberg</b> mountains, where they founded the village later called Schoemansdal.</blockquote><a href="/state/zoutpansberg-republic">Zoutpansberg Republic</a></li></ul></section>
